<article class="article-body">

<p>If you've heard the title Chief of Staff and thought that's just a fancier name for what I already do, I understand why. I thought the same thing before I made the jump myself.</p>

<p>Here's what actually changes.</p>

<p>As an EA, you're mostly reacting. The calendar tells you what matters today. Someone asks for something and you get it done, well, fast, without drama. That's a real skill, don't let anyone tell you otherwise. But the work starts with someone else's request. You're excellent at execution inside a scope someone else has already defined.</p>

<p>As a <a href="/chief-of-staff-kenya/">Chief of Staff</a>, the work starts before the request exists. You're in the room when a decision is still being shaped, not just when it needs executing. You're expected to have an opinion on whether something should happen at all, not just how to make it happen smoothly. Nobody hands you that access. You have to earn it by showing your judgment is solid long before anyone gives you the title.</p>

<p>The shift that mattered most for me wasn't learning a new skill. It was learning to flag a problem before I was asked to fix it.</p>

<h2>Ownership, not tasks</h2>
<p>The cleanest way to see the difference is to look at what each role is actually accountable for. (For the full side-by-side, see <a href="/blog/chief-of-staff-vs-executive-assistant/">Chief of Staff vs Executive Assistant</a>.)</p>

<p>An EA owns the calendar being accurate, the inbox being triaged, the logistics being handled. If those things happen well, the EA has done the job. The scope is bounded and it's bounded on purpose, because that's what makes an EA fast and reliable.</p>

<p>A Chief of Staff owns outcomes that cross the whole business. Did the leadership team actually align on the thing they agreed to last week, or did it quietly die. Is the board pack ready three days early or assembled in a panic the night before. Is there a decision log anywhere, or does the same argument happen again next quarter because nobody wrote down what was decided. None of that is a task you can check off. It's a standard you hold the business to, continuously, whether or not anyone asked you to.</p>

<p>That's the real line. An EA executes against a request. A Chief of Staff owns whether the right things are happening at all.</p>

<h2>What actually earns you the title</h2>
<p>I get asked this constantly, and the honest answer disappoints people who want a checklist. It isn't a certification. It isn't years of tenure. It's a track record of being right about what matters before you're told what matters.</p>

<p>Start small. In your next meeting, don't just take notes on what was said. Notice what wasn't decided, and follow up on it before your executive has to ask. When you see a process breaking two months before it becomes a fire, say so, with a suggested fix attached, not just the warning. When you're handed a task, ask yourself whether the task is actually solving the underlying problem, and say something if it isn't.</p>

<p>None of that requires permission. It requires you to start operating like the job before anyone gives you the title for it. That's how every Chief of Staff I know actually got there, including me.</p>

<h2>The jump isn't about seniority</h2>
<p>A lot of EAs assume the path to Chief of Staff is simply time served. Enough years, enough trust, and the title eventually arrives. It doesn't work that way, and treating it that way is why a lot of capable EAs stay EAs for a decade.</p>

<p>What changes the trajectory is scope. At some point you stop being the person who executes the founder's calendar and start being the person the founder thinks out loud with before a decision is made. That's not a promotion someone grants you for good behavior. It's a shift you initiate, one instance of good judgment at a time, until the executive can't imagine making a call without you in the room.</p>

<p>If you're an EA reading this and wondering whether you have what it takes, the fact that you're asking the question is a decent sign. The EAs who never make the jump usually aren't wondering. They're comfortable in the execution lane, and that's a perfectly good place to build a career. The ones who make the jump are the ones who start acting like owners before the title says they can.</p>

<p>I made this jump myself, from executive assistant to executive business partner to Chief of Staff. If you're navigating the same path and want to talk through where you are, <a href="/booking/">book a free 30-minute call</a>.</p>

